A prop read off a `#id` is answered the same wherever the read is written.

`circle #b(r=a.r)` is a read that only becomes readable once `a`'s own props
are known, so answering runs to a fixed point. A single pass answered only the
reads whose node the traversal had already reached, which made the output
depend on statement order: the `b is 5` below rendered as an empty span when
the `ui.text` came first, and correctly when it came second. A declarative
position has no order to appeal to, so the answer must not have one.

The read is written *before* the canvas here deliberately -- that is the
ordering the single pass got wrong.

deny out/none/html/index.html `\ba\.r\b` -- the chain is answered, not left as a read
deny out/none/html/index.html `<span>\s*</span>` -- an empty span is what the unanswered read rendered

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import draw "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #w(title="t") {
    ui.text(value="b is {b.r}")

    draw.canvas(width=10px, height=10px) {
        draw.circle #a(cx=1.0, cy=1.0, r=5.0)
        draw.circle #b(cx=2.0, cy=2.0, r=a.r)
    }
}
